  <h1>The Suez Crisis: Power, Pressure and Miscalculation</h1>

  <p class="subtitle">
    Understanding the different positions behind the 1956 Suez Crisis,
    and questioning the narratives that followed.
  </p>

  <div class="date">13 November 2026</div>

  <p>
    Historical narratives are often exaggerations of what actually
    happened. The Suez Crisis is one such episode that deserves a more
    careful examination. Rather than immediately reaching conclusions
    about Britain's decline or America's dominance, it is worth
    understanding the different positions and interests involved.
  </p>

  <h2>The American View</h2>

  <p>
    America's primary concern was containing Soviet influence in Europe,
    particularly in strategically important areas such as Greece.
    Washington was also reluctant to support colonial wars.
  </p>

  <p>
    The United States had extended substantial financial assistance and
    credit to European countries, particularly Britain. Its interests
    therefore went beyond simply opposing British policy. It also had
    an interest in preserving European economic stability and maintaining
    a functioning Western alliance during the Cold War.
  </p>

  <h2>The British View</h2>

  <p>
    Britain's position was different. It wanted to maintain its influence
    over the Suez Canal, a strategically important route that it had
    dominated for decades. The nationalisation of the canal by Egypt
    threatened that influence and was viewed as a major strategic and
    political setback.
  </p>

  <p>
    Britain subsequently launched Operation Musketeer alongside France
    and Israel. Militarily, the operation was initially progressing
    favourably. Politically and economically, however, the situation
    became increasingly difficult.
  </p>

  <h2>Could America Simply Force Britain to Stop?</h2>

  <p>
    American financial pressure was significant. Washington threatened
    measures affecting Britain's access to financial support, including
    pressure involving sterling reserves. The prospect of losing further
    financial assistance from institutions such as the IMF also placed
    Britain in a difficult position.
  </p>

  <p>
    But the idea that America could simply pull the plug without
    consequences overlooks several important realities.
  </p>

  <p>
    First, Britain was deeply indebted. A financial collapse would not
    have been a loss for Britain alone. Its creditors, including the
    United States, would also have faced substantial consequences.
    A bankrupt Britain could not easily repay what it owed.
  </p>

  <p>
    Second, Britain possessed a global network of intelligence,
    diplomatic relationships and strategic connections. Its role in
    the security of Gulf countries and other strategically important
    regions was significant. Replacing that network would have taken
    time, resources and institutional capacity.
  </p>

  <p>
    Third, the Cold War depended on alliances. Britain was an important
    bridge between America and Europe. Its diplomatic, military and
    intelligence contributions were valuable to the Western alliance.
    A financially crippled Britain would have been less capable of
    supporting those shared objectives.
  </p>

  <p>
    There was also the question of sterling. Although the Bretton Woods
    system had established the dollar as the central currency of the
    post-war international monetary order, the transition away from
    sterling was not instantaneous. Sterling continued to play an
    important role in international trade and in the reserves of
    countries within the Sterling Area.
  </p>

  <p>
    A British financial collapse would therefore have created wider
    instability, potentially damaging the value of sterling reserves
    held across that network.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    The relationship was not one in which one country could apply
    unlimited pressure without considering the consequences for itself.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Why Didn't America Invade Britain?</h2>

  <p>
    The question may sound exaggerated, but it illustrates a broader
    point: possessing military superiority does not mean that using
    force is always rational.
  </p>

  <p>
    Even if a country has the military capacity to take an action,
    it must consider whether the costs justify the benefits.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    Cost greater than benefit. That is the simplest explanation.
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    America's leverage over Britain was substantial, but its own
    strategic interests placed limits on how far it could sensibly
    push that leverage.
  </p>

  <h2>Britain's Miscalculation</h2>

  <p>
    By 1956, Britain was facing substantial international condemnation
    over the operation. It was under financial pressure and needed
    further loans. Its international position was changing, and it
    needed to reconsider the identity it wanted to project to the world.
  </p>

  <p>
    Britain could have continued further, but the more important
    question was whether doing so was worth the cost.
  </p>

  <p>
    The policy failure of Anthony Eden's government was clear. Britain
    had miscalculated the political and economic consequences of its
    actions. But this was not an experience unique to Britain.
  </p>

  <h2>Superpowers Fail Too</h2>

  <p>
    Consider the United States. Its interventions and foreign-policy
    ambitions have produced major setbacks in Vietnam, Afghanistan
    and Cuba.
  </p>

  <p>
    In the case of Cuba, one might argue that Soviet nuclear capabilities
    made direct military action too dangerous. That is true, but the
    argument must be applied consistently. The United States also
    possessed nuclear weapons. Retaliation and escalation were obvious
    risks for both sides.
  </p>

  <p>
    The existence of nuclear weapons did not automatically make one
    side powerless. It made the cost of escalation potentially
    catastrophic for both.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Soviet Union, too, failed to achieve its objectives in
    Afghanistan. Does that mean it was not a superpower? Of course not.
    A superpower can possess enormous resources and still fail in
    particular conflicts.
  </p>

  <h2>Was Britain No Longer a Superpower?</h2>

  <p>
    This brings us to another question: what exactly defines a
    superpower?
  </p>

  <p>
    There is no single universally accepted definition. Britain's
    position in the twentieth century included several characteristics
    associated with great-power status:
  </p>

  <p>
    It possessed nuclear weapons, maintained a global network of
    military bases and intelligence relationships, and retained the
    capacity to suppress armed rebellions, as demonstrated during
    the Malayan Emergency.
  </p>

  <p>
    Its global empire also continued, in a diminished and changing
    form, into the 1970s. However, possessing these capabilities did
    not mean that Britain could maintain every commitment indefinitely.
  </p>

  <h2>Power Versus the Welfare of Citizens</h2>

  <p>
    Perhaps the more interesting question is not simply whether
    Britain lost power, but what a country should do with its resources.
  </p>

  <p>
    By stepping away from some costly overseas military commitments,
    Britain could redirect attention and resources towards domestic
    priorities. The balance between international influence and the
    welfare of its own population became increasingly important.
  </p>

  <p>
    This raises a comparison with the United States. Despite its
    enormous military and economic resources, Americans continue
    to face substantial healthcare costs, significant student debt
    and unequal access to educational opportunities.
  </p>

  <p>
    Consider the published undergraduate course fees for Home students
    at the University of Oxford for the 2026/27 academic year:
    £9,790 per year. The comparison with American university costs
    raises an important question about the relationship between
    national wealth and the affordability of education.
  </p>

  <h2 class="closing">The Final Question</h2>

  <p>
    Britain was not blameless. The Suez operation was a serious
    strategic miscalculation, and American pressure played a decisive
    role in bringing it to an end.
  </p>

  <p>
    But reducing the entire episode to a story of American command
    and British helplessness misses the larger picture. Financial
    interdependence, intelligence networks, Cold War alliances and
    the costs of escalation all shaped the decisions of the countries
    involved.
  </p>

  <p>
    Superpowers can fail. Powerful countries can miscalculate.
    Military strength does not guarantee political success, and
    international prestige does not necessarily translate into
    a better standard of living for ordinary citizens.
  </p>

  <p>
    Perhaps the real measure of national power should include not
    only what a country can project abroad, but also what it can
    provide for the people living within its borders.
  </p>

